  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Systems Strengthening for One Health Activity</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">(Bangladesh):  The Systems Strengthening for One Health Activity prevents and counters the emergence of infectious disease outbreaks in Bangladesh, and enhances resilience against health-related shocks and stressors. This activity will support the building of a robust global health security architecture to prevent, detect, and respond to health threats at the animal, human, and environmental interface. To reduce the risk of pathogen spillover from domestic and wild animals to humans, this activity will build a collaborative multi-sectoral One Health system at the national and sub-national levels with the Ministries of Fisheries and Livestock; Environment, Forest, and Climate Change; and Health and Family Welfare. It will provide support to functionalize the One Health Secretariat as a multisectoral coordination mechanism. Using FY 2024 funds, the activity will support the government, communities of practices, and private sector to build robust systems to reduce the risk of antimicrobial resistance. It will build laboratory and surveillance systems to seamlessly share information and quickly detect and limit outbreaks. This activity will enhance the government and communities emergency operation and response capacity across the animal, human, and wildlife sectors. It will work with academia and youth to improve skills and knowledge to creatively prevent and mitigate epidemic/pandemic health threats to minimize social and economic disruptions. The activity supports goal three of the Joint Strategic Plan and ICS Mission Objective three. The activity will advance the Indo-Pacific Strategy by working  with GoB to intensify efforts to prevent, detect, and respond to infectious diseases.Funding will contribute to Adaptation Indirect ($1.5m), Digital Technology Indirect ($250k), Gender Equality ($500k), Health Workforce ($700k), PGI-Indirect Digital ($1.5k),PGI-Indirect Health ($1.8m), PSE ($100k) issues.</narrative>
  </description>
